\section{Audit the Axioms Behind an Accepted Theorem}

\Cref{fig:visual-218} distinguishes a kernel-accepted proof from a
kernel-accepted proof that also meets an empty assumption allowlist.

% Explanatory visual
\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[tome diagram]
    \node[tome box] (goal) at (5.2,1.6) {theorem $n+0=n$};
    \node[tome box] (proto) at (2.5,0) {prototype helper};
    \node[tome box] (clean) at (7.9,0) {rebuilt helper};
    \node[tome box,dashed] (a) at (2.5,-1.55) {admitted \texttt{Step}};
    \node[tome box] (p) at (7.9,-1.55) {checked congruence proof};
    \draw[tome arrow] (goal) -- (proto) node[midway,above left] {original};
    \draw[tome arrow] (goal) -- (clean) node[midway,above right] {replacement};
    \draw[tome arrow] (proto) -- (a);
    \draw[tome arrow] (clean) -- (p);
    \node at (2.5,-2.35) {audit rejects};
    \node at (7.9,-2.35) {no admission reached};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Two versions of the same theorem's dependencies. The audit
    walks through the helper to reach the prototype's admitted step.}
  \label{fig:visual-218}
\end{figure}

Kernel acceptance confirms derivability in a particular
environment. A build that promises a theorem without
prototype admissions needs an additional check on that
environment. Take a closed theorem with an accepted proof
term and an immutable library mapping declaration identities
to types, bodies, and declaration kinds. Assume the trusted
interface exposes dependencies of opaque definitions as recorded
when their bodies were checked. Traverse both types and bodies
or those checked dependency records, and compare all reached
assumptions with an explicit allowlist for this build.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Accepted theorem $t$, fixed environment $E$, allowlist $A$
\Let{W}{[t]}
\Let{V}{\varnothing}
\While{$W$ is not empty}
  \Let{d}{\operatorname{pop}(W)}
  \If{$d\notin V$}
    \State Add $d$ to $V$
    \To{Compare declaration identity, not unqualified spelling.}
    \If{$d$ is an assumption and $d\notin A$}
      \State \Return disallowed assumption $d$
    \EndIf
    \To{Opaque bodies still have checked dependencies.}
    \State Enqueue the type and proof dependencies of $d$ in $E$
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\State \Return passed dependency audit
\end{sudocode}

Suppose the theorem $n+0=n$ calls a helper for its
successor case. In a prototype, the helper refers to an
admitted declaration \texttt{Step}. Even though evaluating
the arithmetic at $n=3$ yields $3$ and the kernel accepts
the final proof term in that environment, the audit reaches
\texttt{Step} through the helper and rejects it when the
allowlist is empty. Rebuild with a checked congruence proof
in place of the admission; the dependency walk no longer
finds \texttt{Step}. Treat a missing dependency record as
an incomplete audit, not as an empty list.

Every visited declaration has had each recorded dependency
queued, making a completed traversal closed under reference.
The cost is linear in the reachable declaration graph.
The check says nothing about whether the audited type is the
intended theorem or whether the kernel's reduction rules are
correct. It is also a syntactic audit: an assumption reachable
in the chosen proof might not be needed by a different proof
of the same statement.

\section{Evidence Beyond the Kernel}

When importing a SAT result, one design formalizes a certificate checker as a function and proves that acceptance implies unsatisfiability. The theorem about the checker and the fact that it accepted a particular certificate then justify the result. Merely trusting an external solver's status string is instead a new assumption. Likewise, admissions, imported axioms, conversion rules, and unchecked plugin paths can change the trusted base. Record the theorem's axiom dependencies and rebuild the development without admissions intended only for prototyping.

The kernel also does not establish that an executable is the one covered by a proof of a formal compiler. Berghofer and Strecker's \emph{Extracting a Formally Verified, Fully Executable Compiler from a Proof Assistant} illustrates the bridge from executable Isabelle definitions to ML and their interface with possibly unverified code. Formalized semantics, extraction or code generation, and runtime interfaces all matter when relating a checked theorem to execution. The dossier's \emph{Proof-Carrying IR and Certified Rewrite Contracts} applies the same discipline locally: preserve the exact statement, rule and target semantics together with its checked evidence, so an accepted proof cannot silently license a changed rewrite.
